  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n## Findings\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: Before reading code, I predicted: (1) tooltip would be keyboard-inaccessible due to mouse-only triggers, (2) aria-describedby association between trigger and tooltip content would be missing, (3) the tooltip element would lack an id needed for ARIA referencing. All three predictions confirmed during investigation.\n\n---\n\n### CRITICAL \u2014 Keyboard users cannot access tooltip at all\n\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyTooltip.jsx:8-10` \u2014 only `onMouseEnter` and `onMouseLeave` handlers are present. No keyboard event handlers exist.\n- **ARIA/HTML element**: `<button>` trigger with mouse-only events; no `onFocus`, `onBlur`, or `onKeyDown`.\n- **User group impacted**: Keyboard-only users, motor-impaired users who cannot use a mouse.\n- **Expected behavior (WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip pattern)**: The tooltip must be triggered by both hover AND focus on the trigger element. When the trigger receives keyboard focus, the tooltip should become visible and remain associated via `aria-describedby`.\n- **Fix**: Add `onFocus` and `onBlur` handlers to the button that mirror the mouse behavior. Consider using a shared show/hide function:\n\n```jsx\nconst handleShow = () => setShowTooltip(true);\nconst handleHide = () => setShowTooltip(false);\n\n<button\n  onMouseEnter={handleShow}\n  onMouseLeave={handleHide}\n  onFocus={handleShow}\n  onBlur={handleHide}\n>\n  {trigger}\n</button>\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH \u2014 code contains zero keyboard event handlers. This is not debatable from the source alone.\n- **Why this matters**: Keyboard users (estimated 5-10% of web users) receive no tooltip information at all. The feature exists exclusively for mouse users, which is a fundamental accessibility failure.\n\n---\n\n### MAJOR \u2014 Tooltip content invisible to assistive technology due to missing association\n\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyTooltip.jsx:14` \u2014 the `<div>` has `role=\"tooltip\"` but no `id`. The `<button>` (line 8) has no `aria-describedby` attribute.\n- **ARIA/HTML element**: Missing `id` on tooltip div; missing `aria-describedby` on trigger button.\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver).\n- **Expected behavior (WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value + WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip pattern)**: The trigger must have `aria-describedby` referencing the tooltip's id so screen readers announce the tooltip content when the user focuses the trigger. Without this association, `role=\"tooltip\"` is inert \u2014 it does nothing for assistive technology.\n- **Fix**:\n\n```jsx\nconst tooltipId = React.useId(); // or a stable unique id\n\n<button aria-describedby={tooltipId}>\n  {trigger}\n</button>\n\n{showTooltip && (\n  <div className=\"tooltip-content\" role=\"tooltip\" id={tooltipId}>\n    {content}\n  </div>\n)}\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH \u2014 no `aria-describedby` exists anywhere in the component, and the tooltip div has no `id`.\n- **Why this matters**: Screen reader users hear the button text but never learn what the tooltip says. The entire purpose of a tooltip \u2014 providing supplementary information on demand \u2014 is completely lost for AT users.\n\n---\n\n### MAJOR \u2014 Tooltip element lacks an id, making ARIA association impossible\n\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyTooltip.jsx:14` \u2014 `<div className=\"tooltip-content\" role=\"tooltip\">` has no `id` attribute.\n- **User group impacted**: All assistive technology users (the missing id prevents the aria-describedby fix above from working).\n- **Expected behavior (WAI-ARIA APG)**: The element with `role=\"tooltip\"` must have a unique `id` so it can be referenced by `aria-describedby` on its trigger.\n- **Fix**: Add an `id` to the tooltip div. Use `React.useId()` for automatic uniqueness in React 18+, or generate a stable id if using older React:\n\n```jsx\nconst tooltipId = React.useId();\n// ...\n<div role=\"tooltip\" id={tooltipId}>\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: HIGH \u2014 the element clearly has no `id`.\n- **Why this matters**: This is a prerequisite for the MAJOR finding above. Without an id, aria-describedby cannot point to anything.\n\n---\n\n### MINOR \u2014 No Escape key handling to dismiss tooltip\n\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyTooltip.jsx:8` \u2014 button has no `onKeyDown` handler.\n- **User group impacted**: Keyboard users who open the tooltip via focus and need a way to close it without moving focus away (e.g., when tooltip covers content they need to read).\n- **Expected behavior (WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip pattern)**: Pressing Escape should hide the tooltip and return focus to the trigger.\n- **Fix**:\n\n```jsx\nconst handleKeyDown = (e) => {\n  if (e.key === 'Escape') {\n    setShowTooltip(false);\n    e.target.focus(); // ensure focus stays on trigger\n  }\n};\n\n<button onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}>\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: MEDIUM \u2014 Escape handling is a best practice in the APG pattern but not strictly required if blur hides the tooltip (which onFocus/onBlur would handle). Still worth implementing for completeness.\n- **Why this matters**: Minor friction for keyboard users who may need to dismiss without tabbing away.\n\n---\n\n### ENHANCEMENT \u2014 Tooltip visibility state should use aria-hidden when hidden, not DOM removal\n\n- **Evidence**: `BuggyTooltip.jsx:13` \u2014 uses conditional rendering (`{showTooltip && ...}`) which removes the tooltip from the DOM entirely when hidden.\n- **User group impacted**: Screen reader users in edge cases where rapid show/hide could cause focus or announcement timing issues.\n- **Expected behavior (WAI-ARIA APG)**: Tooltips should remain in the DOM and use `aria-hidden=\"true\"` when not visible, so that screen readers don't encounter disappearing/returning elements during interaction. This also prevents focus from jumping to the tooltip when it appears.\n- **Fix**:\n\n```jsx\n<div\n  className=\"tooltip-content\"\n  role=\"tooltip\"\n  id={tooltipId}\n  aria-hidden={!showTooltip}\n>\n  {content}\n</div>\n```\n\n- **Confidence**: MEDIUM \u2014 conditional rendering works but is less robust than aria-hidden management for interactive components.\n- **Why this matters**: Prevents edge-case screen reader confusion during rapid hover/focus transitions.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing (Gaps)\n\n1. **Keyboard interaction model entirely absent** \u2014 The component has zero keyboard support. This is the most severe gap: a tooltip that only works with a mouse is not a tooltip for half of users.\n2. **Programmatic association between trigger and content** \u2014 No `aria-describedby` / `id` pairing exists. The tooltip's supplementary information is invisible to assistive technology by design, not by accident.\n3. **Focus management on show/hide** \u2014 When the tooltip appears via focus, there is no design for whether focus should remain on the trigger (correct behavior) or move into the tooltip (incorrect). Currently undefined.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n| Perspective | Experience |\n|-------------|-----------|\n| **Screen reader user** | Hears button text. Tooltip content is completely silent \u2014 `role=\"tooltip\"` without `aria-describedby` is invisible to AT. The role attribute alone does nothing. |\n| **Keyboard-only user** | Can focus the button (good). Cannot trigger tooltip at all. No Escape key to dismiss. Tab moves away and tooltip disappears (mouse-leave equivalent doesn't fire on keyboard blur in most cases). Feature is inaccessible. |\n| **Low vision user (200% zoom)** | Tooltip appears on hover \u2014 same as sighted mouse users. But if they use magnifier + keyboard, the feature is unavailable. No contrast or sizing issues identifiable from code alone. |\n| **Cognitive accessibility** | Interaction model is inconsistent: works with mouse, doesn't work with keyboard. This inconsistency itself creates confusion. No indication of what to do for keyboard users. |\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\nThis component fails at the most fundamental level: it provides a feature (supplementary information via tooltip) exclusively to mouse users while being completely inaccessible to keyboard and assistive technology users. The `role=\"tooltip\"` attribute is present but inert \u2014 it's like having a sign that says \"information here\" with nothing behind it.\n\n**What would need to change for ACCEPT**:\n1. Add keyboard event handlers (`onFocus`, `onBlur`) mirroring mouse behavior (CRITICAL fix)\n2. Add `id` to tooltip and `aria-describedby` on trigger button to associate content with trigger (MAJOR fix)\n3. Implement Escape key handling (MINOR fix, recommended)\n\nThese are not complex changes \u2014 they follow the WAI-ARIA APG Tooltip pattern directly. The component needs a targeted revision, not a redesign.\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1. **Tooltip positioning**: Does the tooltip use absolute/fixed positioning that could cause it to be clipped at 200% zoom? This requires visual inspection of CSS.\n2. **Multiple tooltips on page**: If `React.useId()` is not used, multiple instances of this component would produce duplicate ids, breaking aria-describedby associations across the page. Is id uniqueness guaranteed?\n3. **Tooltip duration**: Should the tooltip persist for a minimum time after focus (e.g., 1-2 seconds) to give screen reader users time to read it, or disappear immediately on blur? The current code disappears instantly on mouse leave \u2014 same behavior would apply to keyboard blur, which may be too fast for AT users.",
